Introduction
	The game is about a brave heroine stuck in a never ending tower.
	It is a roguelike (so it is randomly generated, and you lose every items that you find when you die, not that there
	are any items to lose in this version).
	The "rooms" that can be chosen are self made by me, but the order they get chosen is based on the random generator ingame.

Plan
	My goal was to make a succesfull prototype/demo for a roguelike platformer.
	While I've made a physics system before, it was while watching guiding videos. I thought it would be a great idea to finally
	implement it in a game I can be proud to showcase.
	Aditionally, at one of my game interviews for a job I was mentioned how caches are often used in games, so I decided this would be a great opportunity for that.
	Finally, I want to take a more Unity approach and create editor tools and prefabs that potentional designers would use to actually create the levels.
	Using version control for the first time outside of my placement year is also a great idea.

	Okay, lets go over the important features I expaplained above in more detail, from a programming view:
		Physics and Player Controller
			I thought the normal Unity controller is nowhere near good enough for precise platforming, so I decided to make my own. I previously attempted this, but this time I think I got it down pretty well.
			It uses raycasting to detect obstacles, which can be fire either horizontally or vertically.
			It has a state system that tells you exactly what actions the player is doing and what stage that action is in.

		Room EDitor tools
			Rooms. I need to create a lot of those and quick. Especially since they always have a start and exit, plus they are made of many segments.
			So I made a editor tool that allows you to do all of those. Decide were the start and exit are by drag and dropping and add/remove room segments

		ROom cache
			In order to increase performance, rooms arent actually spawned in but rather held in a cache. SPawning a room in involves moving the room to the right position and despawning means moving it back to the cache and reseting the 
			objects inside

